-**Algorithm Steps:**
+**Numbered Steps:**
+1.  **Initial Pass (Statistics Gathering):** Perform a sweep through the volume to gather statistics on handle sizes [S1]. Compute handle sizes for discovered handles (see Step 3) [S1]. Generate a histogram or scatterplot of handle sizes to determine an appropriate threshold $T$ if not provided by the user [S1]. Typical extraneous handles have loop lengths of 4–8 units; $T$ is typically set to 10–20 [S1].
+2.  **Main Sweep and Handle Identification:** Perform an axis-aligned sweep (e.g., along the $z$-axis) through the volume grid [S1]. Incrementally construct and analyze the augmented Reeb graph to represent the topology of the isosurface [S1]. Use a greedy method to isolate geometrically succinct handles, processing one local handle at a time without global access to the entire surface topology [S1].
+3.  **Handle Measurement:** For each handle discovered in the Reeb graph, compute the length of the Reeb loop and the Cross loop using breadth-first searches on the surface connectivity [S1]. The algorithm finds nearly shortest nonseparating cycles rather than globally minimal ones to support out-of-core processing [S1].
+4.  **Handle Removal (Loop Closure):** If the handle size is less than $T$, remove the handle [S1]. Identify the vertices/edges of the Reeb loop on the isosurface [S1]. Construct an approximation to the minimal spanning surface for the nonplanar loop. For efficiency, use a triangle fan centered at the centroid of the loop vertices [S1]. Scan-convert (rasterize) the triangle fan into the volume grid [S1]. Write positive or negative scalar values in the grid cells intersected by the triangle fan, depending on the orientation of the loop. This operation inserts a thin wall, collapsing and pinching off the handle [S1].
